
In Python, opening a file is the initial step to read from or write to a file on your computer.

Purpose: Before reading or writing a file, you must tell Python which file you want to work with and what operations you intend to perform on it.

The open() Function: Opening a file is performed using the built-in open() function in Python.

File Handle: Calling open() returns a file handle—a variable used to perform operations (such as reading, writing, or closing) on the file.

Analogy: It is similar to choosing File -> Open in a word processing program (like Microsoft Word) before you can start reading or editing the document.

Syntax: handle = open(filename, mode)

File Handle: Calling open() returns a handle that you use to manipulate and perform operations on the file.

Filename: The filename parameter is passed as a string (e.g., 'm.txt').

Mode (Optional): Specifies how you intend to use the file:

'r': Read mode — used if you plan to read content from the file.

'w': Write mode — used if you plan to write data to the file.

Core Concepts of File Processing

Text Files vs Binary Files: Text files are structured as sequences of lines ending with newline characters (\n), whereas binary files contain non-text raw bytes (such as images or executable files).

File Handle: An object returned by the open() function that acts as a connection to the file on disk.

File Processing Essentials

File Handle as a Sequence: A file handle opened in read mode acts as an ordered sequence of strings, where each element corresponds to a single line in the file.

